.\" timeout(1) manual page
.\" This is part of the vibeutils project
.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt TIMEOUT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm timeout
.Nd run a command with a time limit
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl s Ar signal
.Op Fl k Ar duration
.Op Fl \-preserve-status
.Op Fl \-foreground
.Op Fl v
.Ar duration
.Ar command
.Op Ar arg ...
.Nm
.Op Fl h | Fl Fl help
.Nm
.Op Fl V | Fl Fl version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility starts
.Ar command
and kills it if it is still running after the specified
.Ar duration .
.Pp
If
.Ar command
times out and
.Fl \-preserve-status
is not set,
.Nm
exits with status 124.
Otherwise it exits with the status of
.Ar command .
If no signal is specified, the
.Dv TERM
signal is sent upon timeout.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl s Ar signal , Fl Fl signal Ns = Ns Ar signal
Send this signal on timeout instead of
.Dv TERM .
.Ar signal
may be a name like
.Dq HUP
or a number like
.Dq 1 .
.It Fl k Ar duration , Fl Fl kill-after Ns = Ns Ar duration
If
.Ar command
is still running after the initial signal, send
.Dv KILL
after this
.Ar duration .
.It Fl \-preserve-status
Exit with the same status as
.Ar command ,
even when it times out.
.It Fl \-foreground
Do not create a separate process group for
.Ar command .
This allows
.Ar command
to use the foreground TTY normally.
.It Fl v , Fl Fl verbose
Diagnose to standard error any signal sent upon timeout.
.It Fl h , Fl Fl help
Display help information and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl Fl version
Output version information and exit.
.El
.Pp
The
.Ar duration
argument is a number with an optional suffix:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy s
Seconds
.Pq default
.It Sy m
Minutes
.It Sy h
Hours
.It Sy d
Days
.El
.Pp
A
.Ar duration
of 0 disables the timeout.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 124
.Ar command
timed out and
.Fl \-preserve-status
is not set.
.It 125
.Nm
itself fails.
.It 126
.Ar command
is found but cannot be invoked.
.It 127
.Ar command
cannot be found.
.It 137
.Ar command
is sent the
.Dv KILL
signal (128+9).
.El
.Pp
Otherwise
.Nm
exits with the status of
.Ar command .
.Sh EXAMPLES
Limit a command to 10 seconds:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ timeout 10 sleep 60
.Ed
.Pp
Limit a build script to 5 minutes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ timeout 5m ./build.sh
.Ed
.Pp
Send KILL instead of TERM on timeout:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ timeout -s KILL 30 long-running-cmd
.Ed
.Pp
Send TERM at 30 seconds, then KILL 5 seconds later if still running:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ timeout -k 5 30 long-running-cmd
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kill 1 ,
.Xr sleep 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility is not specified by POSIX.
It is compatible with the GNU coreutils
.Nm
command.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An "vibeutils implementation by Travis Cole"
